    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "What You Just Experienced",
    "text": "What You Just Experienced\nYour Three Research Modes:\n\n\nMode 1: Without AI\nClassical sources, prior knowledge\nLikely felt: Slower, more effortful, uncertain\n\nMode 2: Single AI Prompt\nOne well-crafted question\nLikely felt: Fast, confident, comprehensive\n\nMode 3: Collaborative AI\nIterative, follow-up questions\nLikely felt: Engaging, interactive, refined\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Critical Question:\n\n\nWhich mode will you remember a week from now?\nThe immediate “feel” doesn’t predict long-term learning…"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#from-15-minutes-to-15-weeks",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#from-15-minutes-to-15-weeks",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "From 15 Minutes to 15 Weeks",
    "text": "From 15 Minutes to 15 Weeks\nWhy Short-Term Experience Misleads:\n\nYour exercise (15 minutes): Different modes felt different\nReal learning (15 weeks): Repeated practice literally rewires your brain\nThe gap: What feels efficient now may prevent expertise later\nThe illusion: AI collaboration felt engaging, but did it build lasting knowledge?\n\n. . .\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWarning\n\n\nKey insight: We optimize for immediate comfort, not long-term capability."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#when-collaborative-ai-helps-vs.-hurts",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#when-collaborative-ai-helps-vs.-hurts",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "When Collaborative AI Helps vs. Hurts",
    "text": "When Collaborative AI Helps vs. Hurts\nThe Nuanced Reality:\n\n\n✅ Collaborative AI Can Help When:\n\nClarifying your thinking: “Does my logic make sense?”\nOvercoming specific obstacles: “I’m stuck on this one step”\nChecking understanding: “Is my interpretation correct?”\nReducing irrelevant load: Grammar, formatting, citations\n\nYou maintain cognitive control\n\n\n\n❌ Collaborative AI Hurts When:\n\nGenerating core ideas: “What should I think about this?”\nDoing the reasoning: “How should I solve this?”\nMaking connections: “How does this relate to…?”\nReplacing struggle: “This is hard, help me”\n\nAI drives the cognitive process\n\n\nThe test: Are you using AI to enhance your thinking or replace it?"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-schools-exist-the-knowledge-divide",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-schools-exist-the-knowledge-divide",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "Why Schools Exist: The Knowledge Divide",
    "text": "Why Schools Exist: The Knowledge Divide\n\n\n🧬 Biologically Primary\nWe evolved to learn:\n\nSpeaking & understanding\nRecognizing faces\n\nReading social cues\nBasic problem solving\n\nNatural discovery works!\n\n\n\n🎓 Biologically Secondary\nWe didn’t evolve to learn:\n\nReading & writing\nMathematics & algebra\nProgramming & analysis\nScientific reasoning\n\nNeeds explicit teaching!\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImportant\n\n\nAI assumes all knowledge is primary—but academic skills need structured struggle."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-learning-journey-novice-expert",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-learning-journey-novice-expert",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "The Learning Journey: Novice → Expert",
    "text": "The Learning Journey: Novice → Expert\n\n\n\n\n\ngraph LR\n    A[😰 Novice&lt;br/&gt;Struggle & Errors] --&gt; B[🤔 Intermediate&lt;br/&gt;Patterns Emerge]\n    B --&gt; C[🎯 Expert&lt;br/&gt;Automatic & Fast]\n    \n    style A fill:#ff6b6b,stroke:#333,stroke-width:3px\n    style B fill:#ffe66d,stroke:#333,stroke-width:3px\n    style C fill:#4ecdc4,stroke:#333,stroke-width:3px\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nNovice: Step-by-step conscious reasoning (high effort)\nIntermediate: Chunking patterns, some automation (moderate effort)\n\nExpert: Instant recognition, can’t explain! (low effort)\n\n\nKey insight: The struggle creates the expertise—you can’t skip it!"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#act-r-theory-how-minds-actually-work",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#act-r-theory-how-minds-actually-work",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "ACT-R Theory: How Minds Actually Work",
    "text": "ACT-R Theory: How Minds Actually Work\nTwo Memory Systems Working Together:\n\n\n🧠 Declarative Memory\n“Knowing That”\n\nChunks: ~3 pieces of info bound together\nFacts: “Paris is capital of France”\nRules: “To isolate x, subtract from both sides”\nLike a network: Connected nodes we search through\n\nSlow, conscious retrieval\n\n\n\n⚡ Procedural Memory\n“Knowing How”\n\nProductions: IF-THEN atomic thinking steps\n“IF see ax = b THEN rewrite as x = b/a”\nModular: Each learned independently\nCompete: Strongest production wins\n\nFast, effortless performance\n\n\nThe learning journey: Chunks → Practice → Productions → Expertise"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#productions-your-brains-if-then-rules",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#productions-your-brains-if-then-rules",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "Productions: Your Brain’s IF-THEN Rules",
    "text": "Productions: Your Brain’s IF-THEN Rules\nProductions are the “atomic thinking steps” of problem-solving:\n\nIF my goal is to solve for x, AND I see equation 3x + 5 = 20, THEN subtract 5 from both sides\nIF I’m stuck on a programming bug, AND code won’t compile, THEN check syntax first\n\nIF I need to remember a phone number, AND can’t write it down, THEN repeat it internally\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nProduction Competition in Action\n\n\nWhen you see 3x + 5 = 20, multiple productions compete:\n\nProduction A: “Guess and check values” (weak, novice)\nProduction B: “Subtract 5 from both sides” (strong, practiced)\nWinner: Strongest production fires automatically\n\nPractice strengthens winning productions!"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-transfer-is-so-limited",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-transfer-is-so-limited",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "Why Transfer Is So Limited",
    "text": "Why Transfer Is So Limited\nScott Young’s Key Insight:\n\n\nTransfer only occurs when skills share productions\n\nChess strategy ≠ Business strategy (no shared productions)\nAlgebra ≠ Geometry (few shared productions)\n\nPython ≠ JavaScript (some shared algorithmic thinking)\n\nThe data confirms: Transfer is linear in number of shared productions!\n\nWhy school feels “useless”:\n\nYou learn 50% of needed productions\nCan’t solve real problems (missing key steps)\nBUT learning the other 50% is now twice as fast!\n\nSchool creates learning potential, not immediate performance"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#knowledge-compilation-in-action",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#knowledge-compilation-in-action",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "Knowledge Compilation in Action",
    "text": "Knowledge Compilation in Action\nHow “atomic thinking steps” become automatic procedures:\n\n\n\n\n\ngraph LR\n    A[📚 Declarative Chunks&lt;br/&gt;\"ax = b means divide&lt;br/&gt;both sides by a\"] --&gt; B[🔄 Practice&lt;br/&gt;Apply rule in&lt;br/&gt;many problems]\n    B --&gt; C[⚡ Production Rule&lt;br/&gt;\"IF see ax = b&lt;br/&gt;THEN answer x = b/a\"]\n    \n    style A fill:#ffe66d,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n    style B fill:#ffb3ba,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n    style C fill:#4ecdc4,stroke:#333,stroke-width:2px\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nStart: Hold rule consciously in working memory (slow, effortful)\nPractice: Each successful use strengthens the production\nCompetition: Stronger productions win over weaker alternatives\n\nCompilation: Direct pattern→response connection forms\nResult: Automatic “atomic thinking step” (fast, effortless)\n\n\nAI provides step 5 without steps 1-4!"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-some-things-are-easy-others-hard",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#why-some-things-are-easy-others-hard",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "Why Some Things Are Easy, Others Hard",
    "text": "Why Some Things Are Easy, Others Hard\nProcedural Memory Examples You Know:\n\nAlphabet forward: Automatic procedural memory → Easy!\nAlphabet backward: Must use declarative memory → Hard!\n\nRecite forward → Hold in working memory → Manually reverse → Say result\n\nTyping your name: Procedural muscle memory → Effortless\nSpelling your name aloud: Declarative retrieval → Requires thought\nPhone number repetition: Rehearsal keeps chunks active in declarative memory\nPhone number from memory: If practiced enough, becomes procedural\n\n. . .\n\n\n\n\n\n\nImportant\n\n\nKey insight: The same information accessed through different memory systems feels completely different to perform!"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-working-memory-bottleneck",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-working-memory-bottleneck",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "The Working Memory Bottleneck",
    "text": "The Working Memory Bottleneck\n\n\n😰 Novice Problem:\n(x + 3)(x - 2) = 0\n7+ pieces to track: - What is x? - Parentheses meaning? - Operations involved? - Why equals zero? - How to solve?\n🧠💥 Overload!\n\n🎯 Expert Problem:\n(x + 3)(x - 2) = 0\n1 pattern recognized: - “Factored form → x = -3 or x = 2”\n🧠✓ Easy!\n\n🤖 AI Problem:\n(x + 3)(x - 2) = 0\n0 processing: - Copy → Paste → Answer\n🧠❌ No learning!"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#how-the-brain-actually-learns",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#how-the-brain-actually-learns",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "How the Brain Actually Learns",
    "text": "How the Brain Actually Learns\nThe Prediction Error System:\n\nBrain predicts: “If I try this approach, it should work”\nReality check: “Did it work as expected?”\nPrediction error: Gap between expected and actual\nNeural update: Brain adjusts based on the error\n\n. . .\n\n\n\n\n\n\nAI Eliminates Learning Signals\n\n\n\nNo predictions (AI provides instant answers)\nNo attempts (students don’t try solutions)\n\nNo errors (AI is always “correct”)\nNo learning (brain has nothing to update)"
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-expertise-illusion",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-expertise-illusion",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "The Expertise Illusion",
    "text": "The Expertise Illusion\n\n\n❌ What AI Creates:\n\nPerfect answers without understanding\nSurface fluency, fragile knowledge\nFalse confidence in abilities\nComplete dependency on tools\n\n\n\n\n✅ What Real Learning Creates:\n\nDeep understanding through struggle\nRobust knowledge that transfers\nAccurate self-assessment\nTrue intellectual autonomy\n\n\n\nThe dangerous mistake: Confusing AI’s performance with your own learning."
  },
  {
    "objectID": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-research-reality-check",
    "href": "slides/how-humans-learn/long/index.html#the-research-reality-check",
    "title": "How We Build Expertise",
    "section": "The Research Reality Check",
    "text": "The Research Reality Check\nRecent studies reveal alarming trends:\n\n68% reduction in critical thinking among high AI users\nSurface learning only in programming students using ChatGPT\n\nFlynn Effect reversal: IQ scores declining since we stopped memorizing\nStudents can’t solve basic problems without AI assistance\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nWarning\n\n\nWe abandoned proven learning methods just as neuroscience proved why they work!"
